      "28 Oct": 1,
      "Saint Anastasia of Rome": 1,
      "According to the Greek life of Saint Anastasia of Rome, she lost her parents at the age of three and was raised in Rome by an abbess named Sophia. During the persecution under Emperor Decius, the city prefect Probus had her arrested. She told him that Jesus Christ was her spouse, her riches and her life, and she was tortured and beheaded. This life was written long after her death, so the details come from tradition. Her feast is October 29 in the Orthodox and Byzantine Catholic calendars. She is not in the current Roman Martyrology, and she is a different saint from Anastasia of Sirmium, whose feast is December 25 in the West.": 1,
      "Brief story based on Saints Encyclopedia. The date follows the Maronite calendar.": 1,
      "Read about Saint Anastasia of Rome": 1,
      "29.10.2026": 1,
      "29 Oct": 1,
      "Saint Macarius of Jerusalem": 1,
      "Macarius was Bishop of Jerusalem from about 312 until his death, which came before the Council of Tyre in 335. He was one of the bishops whom Saint Alexander of Alexandria warned against the teaching of Arius, and at the Council of Nicaea in 325 his name appears first among the bishops of Palestine who subscribed. The chronicler Theophanes relates that after the council the emperor Constantine ordered Macarius to search for the sites of the Resurrection and the Passion and for the True Cross; the Catholic Encyclopedia notes that excavations in Jerusalem began very soon after the council, seemingly under his direction. The Maronite calendar keeps his feast on October 30.": 1,
      "Brief story based on the Catholic Encyclopedia (New Advent). The date follows the Maronite calendar.": 1,
      "Read about Saint Macarius": 1,
